\section{Discussion}
Curvature and dark‑energy dynamics jointly shape the universe’s large‑scale structure. Hyperbolic transformations provide a mathematically consistent method for modeling open‑universe geometries. Numerical stability and autonomous processing are essential for large‑scale simulations.

\section{Conclusion}
This thesis demonstrates the mathematical foundations and physical implications of open‑universe curvature. Hyperbolic geodesic transformations align with FLRW predictions and produce measurable effects on large‑scale structure. Accurate cosmological modeling requires stable numerical methods and comprehensive simulation frameworks.

